WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.680
If barriers are side effects of adapting to different habitats, isolation should

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.680
follow the habitat.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.240
So what happens when fish adapted to the same habitat, but from different lakes,

4
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.480
meet?

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.380
In the monkeyflowers, the strongest early gates were habitat and pollinators.

6
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.140
Were those gates built to keep the species apart?

7
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.040
Or did they come free with adaptation?

8
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.200
One answer is divergent selection, which favours different traits in different

9
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.480
places. Two populations adapt to different habitats, and mating between them falls

10
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.580
as a side effect.

11
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.940
No one designs the barrier.

12
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.280
Selection acts on individuals, and isolation appears along the way.

13
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.600
Isolation that arises like this is called ecological speciation.

14
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:01.243
How could you test it?

15
00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:04.323
Use evolution that repeated itself.

16
00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:10.163
Several small lakes in British Columbia each hold two forms of threespine

17
00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:11.163
stickleback.

18
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:13.957
The benthic form feeds on the bottom.

19
00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:17.397
The limnetic form feeds in open water.

20
00:01:18.737 --> 00:01:21.457
Each lake's pair is thought to have arisen on its own.

21
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:28.010
Rundle, Schluter and colleagues ran hundreds of laboratory mating trials, with fish

22
00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:33.630
from three lakes. They paired fish of the same and different forms, from the same

23
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.630
and different lakes.

24
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.740
A benthic fish from one lake meets a benthic fish from another lake.

25
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.860
Their ancestors split apart separately.

26
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.240
Will they mate?

27
00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:57.347
Yes. Same-form pairs from different lakes showed no strong isolation.

28
00:01:58.707 --> 00:02:03.327
But benthic and limnetic fish were strongly isolated, whichever lakes they came

29
00:02:03.327 --> 00:02:04.327
from.

30
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.770
Look at what each idea predicted.

31
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.170
If ancestry mattered, fish from one lake would mate best.

32
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.950
If ecology mattered, fish of one form would mate best, wherever they came from.

33
00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:21.613
So isolation followed ecology, not ancestry.

34
00:02:23.233 --> 00:02:27.273
Drift alone would be unlikely to repeat the same pattern lake after lake.

35
00:02:28.613 --> 00:02:30.813
Divergent selection is the likely cause.

36
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:33.763
Now return to the monkeyflowers.

37
00:02:34.963 --> 00:02:39.323
The red one grows mainly at lower elevations, the pink one higher up.

38
00:02:40.523 --> 00:02:42.363
Their flowers suit different pollinators.

39
00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:48.833
The pink flower is wide, with a small nectar reward, and bumblebees visit it.

40
00:02:49.893 --> 00:02:54.473
The red flower has a narrow tube and a large reward, and hummingbirds visit it.

41
00:02:55.617 --> 00:02:58.297
Those adaptations are themselves the gates.

42
00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:02.537
Plants adapted to different elevations rarely meet.

43
00:03:03.677 --> 00:03:07.777
A pollinator that favours one flower carries little pollen to the other.

44
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.910
Fish or flowers, the lesson is the same.

45
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:19.190
Adapt to different places and partners, and isolation often grows as a side effect.
